      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "Dissociation of Episodic Content and Identity",
          "body": "R.B.'s case supports a page on how apparently necessary links between episodic recollection and numerical personal identity can turn out to be contingent. The memory evidence says autobiographical event content, factual self-knowledge, trait self-knowledge, voluntary recall, and some future planning can persist while the felt mineness normally taken to certify identity is absent."
        },
        {
          "title": "Essence, Necessity, and Explanation",
          "body": "The grounding and essence sources provide a contrast class for this contingency claim. Trogdon's account treats full grounding as metaphysically necessitating what is grounded through essence, while Correia and Skiles argue that generalized identity can distinguish genuine essence from merely necessary entailment or coextension."
        },
        {
          "title": "Synthesis Constraint",
          "body": "The synthesis should be careful: the memory source does not present a formal grounding theory, and the grounding sources do not analyze episodic memory. The shared topic is that identity-involving appearances may need decomposition before they can support claims of essence-like necessity."
        }
      ],
