        {
          "title": "Psychological Conditions on Metaphysical Commitment",
          "body": "The memory and psychedelic-belief sources jointly support a topic on how metaphysical commitments may be shaped by contingent psychological systems rather than by argument alone. In the memory case, ownership feelings affect the evidential force of memory for personal identity; in the psychedelic studies, ceremonies and psilocybin therapy are associated with shifts away from hard materialism."
        },
        {
          "title": "Mediating Mechanisms",
          "body": "The shared concept is mediation. R.B.'s dissociation suggests that the sense of diachronic identity depends on mechanisms supplying mineness, while the psychedelic source reports emotional synchrony and baseline peer conformity as predictors or mediators of later non-physicalist belief change."
        },
        {
          "title": "Epistemic Rather Than Refutational Force",
          "body": "The topic should not claim that these mechanisms refute the metaphysical views involved. The stronger supported point is epistemic: local evidence suggests that some intuitions or commitments about selfhood, physicalism, and identity are sensitive to cognitive and social conditions."
        }
      ],
