        {
          "title": "Relations Without Simple Priority",
          "body": "R.B.'s unowned recollections create a natural bridge to debates about whether metaphysical relations must have strict-order structure. The memory source pressures simple views that episodic memory automatically grounds or guarantees personal identity, because apparent memory content can remain without felt ownership."
        },
        {
          "title": "Grounding Beyond Strict Order",
          "body": "The grounding-order source similarly challenges structural assumptions about grounding by arguing that grounding is not transitive, irreflexive, or asymmetric. Its truthmaking and alethic-fact grounding examples are used to show that intuitive direction or priority does not by itself settle the formal profile of grounding."
        },
        {
          "title": "Comparative Use",
          "body": "The cross-source synthesis is limited but useful: both sources warn against building metaphysical theory around an over-simple relational template. A page can compare identity-through-memory and ground-grounded relations as cases where intuitive direction, priority, or evidential force needs finer analysis."
        }
